\section{Rows the variance is computed from}{

A robust or cluster-robust variance pairs each observation's score with
its cluster, so it is computed from the rows the model was fitted on:
the model frame the fit keeps (\code{lm}, \code{glm}, or any fit with
\code{model = TRUE}), \code{glm}'s stored data, or the data object as it is now
when its model frame reproduces the fit exactly (its response, weights
and linear predictor, or the stored frame column for column). A \code{coxph}
or \code{survreg} fit keeps no data by default, so if its data frame is
re-sorted, filtered or edited after fitting, a variance that needs the
rows (a cluster variable, the grouped dfbeta sandwich, the clusters of
a \code{cluster()} term) is an error: refit on the current data or with
\code{model = TRUE}. A cluster variable that is not in the model frame is
read the same way; a vector must be in the order of the fitted
observations. A robust \code{coxph} fit's own sandwich (weighted, \code{id},
\code{cluster()}) is stored at fit time and needs no data.

After a weighted fit, pass the estimation weights to \code{marginaleffects}
as \code{wts}: \code{tt_vcov()} returns the variance only, not the weights.
}

